The outcome vocabulary is CLOSED, NAMED, SHOWN IN THE SOURCE as a constant, and exactly one value is recorded for every candidate error, always. Eight words, and the operator has settled that there is no ninth: `opened`, `commented`, `deduped-open`, `skipped-disabled`, `skipped-no-remote`, `skipped-gh-missing`, `skipped-unauthenticated`, `failed`. Silence is not one of them. THE MAPPING FROM SCENARIO TO WORD IS A TOTAL FUNCTION AND IS WRITTEN DOWN HERE, because a vocabulary that is referenced but never shown is one the writer reinvents; the decision procedure runs in this order and stops at the first line that matches:
  1. the project's `error_issues` switch resolves off -> `skipped-disabled` (reason `switch-off`);
  2. `resolveProjectRepo` refuses -> `skipped-no-remote`, and the refusal's own name is the reason (`no-checkout` | `not-a-repo` | `no-origin` | `unparseable-remote` | `non-github-host`);
  3. `gh` classifies as missing -> `skipped-gh-missing` (reason `gh-not-on-path`); as unauthenticated -> `skipped-unauthenticated` (reason `gh-unauthenticated`); as unknown -> `failed` (reason `gh-unknown`);
  4. the log already carries an `issue-reported` for this (project, fingerprint) whose recorded `latest_event_id` equals this candidate's newest contributing event id -- nothing has happened since we last reported -> `deduped-open` (reason `already-reported`), and NO network call is made;
  5. the search runner exits non-zero or returns unparseable JSON -> `failed` (reason `search-failed`);
  6. the search returns an open issue whose body carries the exact fingerprint line -> comment; the comment succeeds -> `commented`, it fails -> `failed` (reason `comment-failed`);
  7. otherwise create; the create succeeds -> `opened`, it fails -> `failed` (reason `create-failed`).
Every scenario reaches exactly one word and every word is reached by at least one scenario. `reason` is REQUIRED on every outcome except `opened` and `commented`, which instead require `issue_url`; the reason vocabulary above is a closed set exported beside the outcome set. `failed` is deliberately ONE word with three reasons rather than three words: the operator's remedy for a search failure, a comment failure and a create failure is the same -- look at the reason -- and splitting the word would put the same fact in two places. D-133's rule governs: a silent skip and a silent failure are indistinguishable unless the run records which one happened.
